            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the three *forbidden directions* are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{x}&:\\text{horizontal lines }y=c,\\\\\nd_{y}&:\\text{vertical lines }x=c,\\\\\nd_{-1}&:\\text{lines of slope }-1,\\;x+y=c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2.1 (Reduction to one line of each forbidden direction)\n\n*Statement.*  \nGiven any collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, there exists another collection \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of at most \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the same covering condition such that\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains **exactly one** horizontal line, **exactly one** vertical line and **exactly one** line of slope \\(-1\\);\n* the set of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is the same as in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains a line of each forbidden direction, delete all but one line of each direction; the deleted lines are parallel to a remaining line of the same direction, hence every point they covered is still covered by the remaining line.  \n\nIf a direction, say horizontal, is missing, pick any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (such a point exists, e.g. \\((1,1)\\)). Since the covering condition holds, some line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\). Replace \\(L\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); this new line still passes through \\((a,b)\\) and therefore does not destroy the covering of any other required point (the only points that could be lost are those that were covered *only* by \\(L\\); but those points also lie on the new horizontal line because they have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\)). Thus we may add a horizontal line without harming the condition. The same argument works for the vertical and diagonal directions. Repeating the process yields a collection with exactly one line of each forbidden direction and no change in the set of sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 3.1 (Freedom of the remaining lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) and any multiset \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(m\\) lines, each of which is parallel to one of \\(H,V,D\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C = H\\cup V\\cup D\\cup\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}L\n\\]\n\ncontains every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) **provided** \\(m=n-3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in V\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).  \n\nHence we may assume \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). Write  \n\n\\[\na+b = 2+t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(t\\le n-1\\). The line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y = 2+t\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) and passes through \\((a,b)\\). Thus every point with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on a diagonal line parallel to \\(D\\) whose constant term is \\(2+t\\) for some integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).\n\nThe values \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=3\\) correspond respectively to the lines \\(x+y=4\\) and \\(x+y=5\\); however the points with \\(t=2\\) (i.e. \\(a+b=4\\)) already include the cases \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) and are therefore covered by \\(H\\) or \\(V\\). Similarly for \\(t=3\\) (i.e. \\(a+b=5\\)) at least one of the coordinates equals \\(1\\) for the smallest points, which are again covered. Consequently the only values of \\(t\\) that are *not* already covered by \\(H\\) or \\(V\\) are  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{4,5,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\((n-1)-4+1 = n-4\\) values. Adding the line \\(D\\) itself (corresponding to \\(t=0\\)) we obtain a total of \\(n-3\\) diagonal lines needed to cover all remaining points.\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal F| = n-3\\), we can assign the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) precisely to the required constants \\(2+t\\) for each \\(t\\) in the above set (or, equivalently, to any collection of \\(n-3\\) lines parallel to \\(H,V\\) or \\(D\\)). Hence every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Determination of admissible numbers of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n1. By Lemma\u202f2.1 we may assume that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly one horizontal line \\(H\\), one vertical line \\(V\\) and one diagonal line \\(D\\) (all three are non\u2011sunny).  \n\n2. The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are unrestricted: Lemma\u202f3.1 shows that any choice of \\(n-3\\) lines parallel to \\(H\\), \\(V\\) or \\(D\\) still covers all required lattice points. Therefore the only restriction on the number of sunny lines comes from the fact that at most the \\(n-3\\) *free* lines can be sunny.\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\nConversely, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we construct a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\))\n\n1. **Three mandatory non\u2011sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines** \u2013 choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For concreteness set, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (i+2) .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq 0,-1\\) and is not vertical, so it is sunny. The lines are distinct because both slope and intercept differ.\n\n3. **Remaining non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013 we need \\(n-3-k\\) further lines, each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   Choose them arbitrarily, for example:\n\n   * horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots , y= h\\);\n   * vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots , x= v\\);\n   * diagonal lines \\(x+y=3, x+y=4,\\dots , x+y= d\\),\n\n   where the total number of chosen lines equals \\(n-3-k\\). (If necessary we mix the three families; any selection works.)\n\nAll together we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(H)+1\\;(V)+1\\;(D)+k\\;(S_i)+(n-3-k)\\;(\\text{extra non\u2011sunny}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, the other \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3.1, the set consisting of \\(H,V,D\\) together with the \\(n-3-k\\) extra non\u2011sunny lines covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Adding the sunny lines does not remove any points, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).** The construction yields only the three mandatory non\u2011sunny lines; there is no room for a sunny line, so the only admissible value is \\(k=0\\). This agrees with the formula \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-3\\}=\\{0\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n-3\\).** All free lines are taken sunny; the three mandatory lines are the only non\u2011sunny ones. The construction above with the extra non\u2011sunny set empty gives a valid configuration.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).** All free lines are chosen among the three forbidden directions; the configuration consists solely of non\u2011sunny lines and satisfies the covering condition by Lemma\u202f3.1.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the covering condition forces the presence of at least one line in each of the three forbidden directions, leaving exactly \\(n-3\\) lines that may be chosen arbitrarily. Consequently the number of sunny lines can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-3\\), and for each such integer an explicit configuration exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
