 only values of\u202ft\u202fthat are *not* already covered by\u202fH\u202for\u202fV are\u202ft\u202f\u2208\u202f{4,5,\u2026,n\u20111}, which contains exactly\u202f(n\u20111)\u20114\u202f+\u202f1\u202f=\u202fn\u20114\u202fvalues. Adding the line\u202fD\u202fitself (corresponding to\u202ft\u202f=\u202f0) we obtain a total of\u202fn\u20113\u202fdiagonal lines needed\u2026\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The counting is wrong: to cover all points with\u202fa,b\u202f\u2265\u202f2\u202fone needs diagonal lines for every\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2,\u2026,\u202fn\u20111\u202f(i.e.\u202fn\u20112\u202flines), not only for\u202ft\u202f\u2265\u202f4. The argument that only\u202fn\u20113\u202fextra lines suffice is false.\n\n* **Location:** Construction (Section \u201cConstruction for a given\u202fk\u201d) \u2013 \u201cBy Lemma\u202f3.1, the set consisting of\u202fH,\u202fV,\u202fD\u202ftogether with the\u202fn\u20113\u2011k\u202fextra non\u2011sunny lines covers every lattice point\u202f(a,b)\u202fwith\u202fa,b\u202f\u2265\u202f1\u202fand\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f+\u202f1.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This claim rests on Lemma\u202f3.1, which is invalid; consequently the construction does not guarantee the required covering condition.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### 1. Definition of \u201csunny\u201d lines\n> \u201cA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from\u202f0,\u202f\u221e\u202fand\u202f\u22121.\u201d\n\n*Correct.* The three forbidden directions correspond precisely to horizontal, vertical, and lines of slope\u202f\u22121 (i.e.\u202fx\u202f+\u202fy\u202f=\u202fc). No issue here.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemma\u202f2.1 \u2013 Reduction to one line of each forbidden direction\n\n#### Statement\n> \u201cGiven any collection\u202f\u2112\u202fof\u202fn\u202fdistinct lines satisfying the covering condition, there exists another collection\u202f\u2112\u2032\u202fof at most\u202fn\u202fdistinct lines satisfying the same covering condition such that  \n> \u2013\u202f\u2112\u2032\u202fcontains **exactly one** horizontal line, **exactly one** vertical line and **exactly one** line of slope\u202f\u22121;  \n> \u2013\u202fthe set of sunny lines in\u202f\u2112\u2032\u202fis the same as in\u202f\u2112.\u201d\n\n#### Proof \u2013 First part (deleting extra parallel lines)\n> \u201cIf\u202f\u2112\u202falready contains a line of each forbidden direction, delete all but one line of each direction; the deleted lines are parallel to a remaining line of the same direction, hence every point they covered is still covered by the remaining line.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nParallel lines have distinct intercepts; a point lying on a deleted line need not lie on the remaining parallel line. For example, if \u2112 contains the horizontal lines\u202fy\u202f=\u202f1\u202fand\u202fy\u202f=\u202f2, deleting\u202fy\u202f=\u202f2\u202fremoves coverage of all points with\u202fy\u202f=\u202f2, which are not covered by\u202fy\u202f=\u202f1. Hence the claim that \u201cevery point they covered is still covered\u201d is false. This invalidates the reduction step.\n\n#### Proof \u2013 Second part (adding a missing direction)\n> \u201cIf a direction, say horizontal, is missing, pick any lattice point\u202f(a,b)\u202fwith\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f+\u202f1\u202f(e.g.\u202f(1,1)). Since the covering condition holds, some line\u202fL\u202f\u2208\u202f\u2112\u202fpasses through\u202f(a,b). Replace\u202fL\u202fby the horizontal line\u202fy\u202f=\u202fb; this new line still passes through\u202f(a,b)\u202fand therefore does not destroy the covering of any other required point (the only points that could be lost are those that were covered *only* by\u202fL; but those points also lie on the new horizontal line because they have the same\u202fy\u2011coordinate\u202fb).\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nA line\u202fL\u202fthat passes through\u202f(a,b)\u202fgenerally contains many other lattice points with different\u202fy\u2011coordinates. Replacing\u202fL\u202fby\u202fy\u202f=\u202fb\u202fremoves all those points from the covering set unless they happen to lie on another line of the new configuration. The argument that \u201cthose points also lie on the new horizontal line because they have the same\u202fy\u2011coordinate\u202fb\u201d is incorrect; only the point\u202f(a,b)\u202fhas that\u202fy\u2011coordinate. Consequently the replacement can destroy the covering condition.\n\nBecause both sub\u2011arguments of Lemma\u202f2.1 are invalid, the lemma itself is false. All subsequent results that rely on it (including Lemma\u202f3.1\u2019s \u201cwithout loss of generality\u201d assumption) are therefore unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemma\u202f3.1 \u2013 \u201cFreedom of the remaining lines\u201d\n\n#### Statement\n> \u201cLet  \n> H:\u202fy\u202f=\u202f1,\u2003V:\u202fx\u202f=\u202f1,\u2003D:\u202fx\u202f+\u202fy\u202f=\u202f2.  \n> For any integer\u202fm\u202f\u2265\u202f0 and any multiset\u202f\ud835\udd3d\u202fof\u202fm\u202flines, each of which is parallel to one of\u202fH,\u202fV,\u202fD, the set  \n> \ud835\udc9e\u202f=\u202fH\u202f\u222a\u202fV\u202f\u222a\u202fD\u202f\u222a\u202f\u22c3_{L\u2208\ud835\udd3d}\u202fL  \n> contains every lattice point\u202f(a,b)\u202fwith\u202fa,b\u202f\u2265\u202f1\u202fand\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f+\u202f1\u202fprovided\u202fm\u202f=\u202fn\u202f\u2212\u202f3.\u201d\n\n#### Proof \u2013 Coverage of points with a,b\u202f\u2265\u202f2\n> \u201cIf\u202fa\u202f=\u202f1\u202fthen\u202f(a,b)\u202f\u2208\u202fV. If\u202fb\u202f=\u202f1\u202fthen\u202f(a,b)\u202f\u2208\u202fH. Hence we may assume\u202fa\u202f\u2265\u202f2\u202fand\u202fb\u202f\u2265\u202f2. Write\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f=\u202f2\u202f+\u202ft\u202fwith\u202ft\u202f\u2265\u202f2. Because\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f+\u202f1, we have\u202ft\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f\u2212\u202f1. The line\u202fL_t:\u202fx\u202f+\u202fy\u202f=\u202f2\u202f+\u202ft\u202fhas slope\u202f\u22121 and passes through\u202f(a,b). Thus every point with\u202fa,b\u202f\u2265\u202f2\u202flies on a diagonal line parallel to\u202fD\u202fwhose constant term is\u202f2\u202f+\u202ft\u202ffor some integer\u202ft\u202fwith\u202f2\u202f\u2264\u202ft\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f\u2212\u202f1.\u201d\n\n*This part is correct:* any point (a,b) indeed lies on the line x+y = a+b = 2+t.\n\n#### Proof \u2013 Claim about t\u202f=\u202f2 and t\u202f=\u202f3\n> \u201cThe values\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2 and\u202ft\u202f=\u202f3 correspond respectively to the lines\u202fx\u202f+\u202fy\u202f=\u202f4\u202fand\u202fx\u202f+\u202fy\u202f=\u202f5; however the points with\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2 (i.e.\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f=\u202f4) already include the cases\u202fa\u202f=\u202f1\u202for\u202fb\u202f=\u202f1 and are therefore covered by\u202fH\u202for\u202fV. Similarly for\u202ft\u202f=\u202f3 (i.e.\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f=\u202f5) at least one of the coordinates equals\u202f1\u202ffor the smallest points, which are again covered.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nThe set of points with\u202fa\u202f+\u202fb\u202f=\u202f4\u202fcontains (2,2), (3,1), (1,3). While (3,1) and (1,3) are indeed covered by\u202fV\u202for\u202fH, the point (2,2) has\u202fa,b\u202f\u2265\u202f2\u202fand is **not** on\u202fH\u202for\u202fV. Hence the claim that *all* points with\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2 are already covered is false. The same mistake occurs for\u202ft\u202f=\u202f3: points (2,3) and (3,2) are not covered by\u202fH\u202for\u202fV. Therefore the argument that diagonal lines for\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2,3 are unnecessary is invalid.\n\n#### Proof \u2013 Counting of needed diagonal lines\n> \u201cConsequently the only values of\u202ft\u202fthat are *not* already covered by\u202fH\u202for\u202fV are\u202ft\u202f\u2208\u202f{4,5,\u2026,n\u20111}, which contains exactly\u202f(n\u20111)\u20114\u202f+\u202f1\u202f=\u202fn\u20114\u202fvalues. Adding the line\u202fD\u202fitself (corresponding to\u202ft\u202f=\u202f0) we obtain a total of\u202fn\u20113\u202fdiagonal lines needed to cover all remaining points.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nBecause the previous claim about\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2,3 is false, the set of uncovered\u202ft\u2011values actually includes\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2\u202fand\u202ft\u202f=\u202f3. The correct set of required diagonal lines is\u202ft\u202f=\u202f2,3,\u2026,\u202fn\u20111, i.e.\u202fn\u20112\u202fdifferent diagonal lines (including\u202fD\u202ffor\u202ft\u202f=\u202f0). The counting \u201cn\u20114\u202f+\u202f1 = n\u20113\u201d is therefore incorrect. Consequently the lemma\u2019s conclusion that exactly\u202fn\u20113\u202fextra lines (parallel to H, V, or D) suffice is unfounded.\n\nSince Lemma\u202f3.1 is based on the erroneous claim above, the lemma itself is false.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Determination of admissible numbers of sunny lines\n\n> \u201c1. By Lemma\u202f2.1 we may assume that\u202f\u2112\u202fcontains exactly one horizontal line\u202fH, one vertical line\u202fV and one diagonal line\u202fD (all three are non\u2011sunny).  \n> 2. The remaining\u202fn\u20113\u202flines of\u202f\u2112\u202fare unrestricted: Lemma\u202f3.1 shows that any choice of\u202fn\u20113\u202flines parallel to\u202fH,\u202fV\u202for\u202fD\u202fstill covers all required lattice points. Therefore the only restriction on the number of sunny lines comes from the fact that at most the\u202fn\u20113\u202f*free* lines can be sunny. Hence\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20113.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nBoth Lemma\u202f2.1 and Lemma\u202f3.1 are invalid, so the reduction to \u201cexactly one line of each forbidden direction\u201d and the claim that the remaining\u202fn\u20113\u202flines can be arbitrary (including sunny) are unsupported. The inequality\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20113 is not justified.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for a given\u202fk (0\u202f\u2264\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20113)\n\n> \u201c1. Three mandatory non\u2011sunny lines H, V, D.  \n> 2. Sunny lines S_i:\u202fy\u202f=\u202fi\u202fx\u202f+\u202f(i\u202f+\u202f2) for\u202fi\u202f=\u202f1,\u2026,k.  \n> 3. Remaining non\u2011sunny lines: choose\u202fn\u20113\u2011k\u202ffurther lines parallel to H, V, or D.  \n> By Lemma\u202f3.1, the set consisting of\u202fH,\u202fV,\u202fD together with the\u202fn\u20113\u2011k\u202fextra non\u2011sunny lines covers every lattice point (a,b) with a,b\u202f\u2265\u202f1 and a\u202f+\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u202f+\u202f1. Adding the sunny lines does not remove any points, so the covering condition is satisfied.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nThe claim \u201cBy Lemma\u202f3.1 \u2026 covers every lattice point\u201d relies on Lemma\u202f3.1, which is false. Consequently the construction does **not** guarantee that all required points are covered. The argument that adding sunny lines cannot destroy coverage is correct, but without a valid covering set to start from the construction fails.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Edge Cases\n\n> \u201cn\u202f=\u202f3 \u2192 only the three mandatory non\u2011sunny lines, so k\u202f=\u202f0.  \n> k\u202f=\u202fn\u20113 \u2192 all free lines are sunny, etc.\u201d\n\n**Issue (Critical Error).**  \nThese conclusions are derived from the flawed reduction and construction. Since the underlying lemmas are invalid, the edge\u2011case analysis is not substantiated.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Conclusion\n\nThe solution\u2019s core reduction (Lemma\u202f2.1) and the covering argument (Lemma\u202f3.1) are both incorrect. Because the final answer (that the admissible values of\u202fk are\u202f0,1,\u2026,n\u20113) rests entirely on these faulty lemmas, the proof does not establish the claim. Therefore the solution is **invalid**."
