ertical) and distinct from each other and from the three original non\u2011sunny lines because their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different from \\(1\\).\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Verification of the construction\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe three forced lines have different directions.  \nThe sunny lines have pairwise different slopes, hence cannot coincide with any of the forced lines nor with each other.  \nThe additional vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, none equal to \\(1\\); therefore they are distinct from each other and from the forced vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) is either  \n\n* one of the three points \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (covered by the forced lines),  \n* one of the points \\((a_j,b_j)\\) chosen for a sunny line (covered by that sunny line), or  \n* a point that remained uncovered after the previous steps.  \n\nFor the latter case we assigned a vertical line \\(x=c\\) that passes through it; consequently it is covered.  \nHence the union of all \\(n\\) lines contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); all other lines are horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  Therefore the configuration has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  Edge cases\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three forced lines are \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\); they already cover the three points, so we may take \\(k=0\\).  \nIf we replace the line \\(x+y=4\\) by a sunny line, e.g. the line through \\((2,1)\\) with slope \\(1/2\\), we obtain a configuration with \\(k=1=n-2\\).  \nThus the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1\\}