            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 0.\u202fNotation and preliminary observations  \n\n* A line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\\{\\,\\text{horizontal},\\ \\text{vertical},\\ \\text{line }x+y=0\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* For a line \\(\\ell\\) write  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C(\\ell)=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1,\\ (a,b)\\in\\ell\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* The family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) must satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  \\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\mathcal C(\\ell_{i})=\n  \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}. \\tag{0.1}\n  \\]\n\n* The set on the right\u2013hand side contains exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  N:=\\sum_{s=2}^{n} (s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n  \\]  \n\n  points (for each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) there are \\(s-1\\) choices).\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 1.\u202fUpper bound: at most \\(n-3\\) sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f1.** *In any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines at least three of them are non\u2011sunny; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-3\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines cover at most one point.**  \n   *If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis it has equation \\(y=c\\). All points of (0.1) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so such a line can contain at most one of them.*  \n   *The same argument works for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)).*  \n   *A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). The points of (0.1) have distinct values of \\(a+b\\); hence at most one point lies on a given line of this family.*  \n\n   Therefore each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one point to the covering.\n\n2. **A sunny line contains at most two points.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be sunny, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Suppose \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) of (0.1).  \n   The three points determine \\(\\ell\\); writing the equation through \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) gives slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Using the third point we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_{3}-b_{1}}{a_{3}-a_{1}}=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting the two equalities yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (b_{2}-b_{1})(a_{3}-a_{1})=(b_{3}-b_{1})(a_{2}-a_{1}) .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_{2}+b_{2})-(a_{1}+b_{1})=(a_{3}+b_{3})-(a_{1}+b_{1}) .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(a_{2}+b_{2}=a_{3}+b_{3}\\). Because the three points have distinct sums \\(a+b\\) (the sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(n\\) and each occurs at most once on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), we must have \\(a_{2}+b_{2}=a_{3}+b_{3}\\) only when the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(m=-1\\), contradicting sunniness. Therefore a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of (0.1); it can contain at most two.\n\n3. **Counting.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines. The non\u2011sunny lines contribute at most three points, so the sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   N-3=\\frac{n(n-1)}2-3\n   \\]  \n\n   points. Since each sunny line covers at most two points, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   2s\\ge N-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(N-3>2(n-2)\\); consequently \\(s\\le n-3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2.\u202fConstruction attaining the upper bound  \n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (0.1) and containing exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\n### 2.1\u202fThree non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_{2}:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_{3}:\\;x+y=n .\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) contains the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\).  \n* \\(L_{2}\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \n* \\(L_{3}\\) contains the points \\((a,n-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\).\n\nThus the three lines cover all points of (0.1) whose first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\) or whose sum equals \\(n\\).\n\n### 2.2\u202fSunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_{t}:\\; y=-\\frac{t}{\\,t-1\\,}\\,x+\\frac{t(t+1)}{\\,t-1\\,}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(U_{t}\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((t,2)\\) and \\((2,t)\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny\\bigl|_{x=t}&=-\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,t+\\frac{t(t+1)}{t-1}\n               =\\frac{-t^{2}+t^{2}+t}{t-1}=2,\\\\[2mm]\ny\\bigl|_{x=2}&=-\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,2+\\frac{t(t+1)}{t-1}\n               =\\frac{-2t+t^{2}+t}{t-1}=t .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) because \\(t\\le n-2\\).  \n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(U_{t}\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{t}{t-1}\\). For any integer \\(t\\ge2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{t}{t-1}\\neq0,\\qquad -\\frac{t}{t-1}\\neq\\infty,\\qquad \n-\\frac{t}{t-1}\\neq-1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(U_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe lines \\(U_{t}\\) are distinct because distinct values of \\(t\\) give distinct slopes.\n\n### 2.3\u202fVerification of covering  \n\n*Points already covered.*  \nAll points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or with sum \\(n\\) are on \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a point of (0.1) not covered yet. Then \\(a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n-1\\).  \nIf \\(a=2\\) (resp. \\(b=2\\)) then \\(b\\le n-3\\) (resp. \\(a\\le n-3\\)), so \\(b\\) (resp. \\(a\\)) lies in the interval \\([2,n-2]\\). Choose \\(t=b\\) (resp. \\(t=a\\)); then \\((a,b)\\) equals \\((2,t)\\) (resp. \\((t,2)\\)) and lies on \\(U_{t}\\).  \n\nThus every point of (0.1) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\n\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\cup\\{U_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) are non\u2011sunny, the \\(n-3\\) lines \\(U_{t}\\) are sunny, and all lines are distinct. Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3.\u202fSunny\u2011to\u2011non\u2011sunny replacement  \n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (replacement)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (0.1) and containing at least one sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Then there exists a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\ell'\\notin\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still satisfies (0.1).\n\nConsequently the number of sunny lines can be reduced by one.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, its slope \\(m\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). By Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of the set (0.1). Denote these points (possibly coincident) by  \n\n\\[\nP=(a_{1},b_{1}),\\qquad Q=(a_{2},b_{2}).\n\\]\n\nThree mutually exclusive situations occur.\n\n1. **Both points have the same second coordinate** (\\(b_{1}=b_{2}\\)).  \n   Replace \\(\\ell\\) by the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell' :\\; y=b_{1}.\n   \\]\n\n   This line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n   Every point of (0.1) with second coordinate \\(b_{1}\\) is already covered by \\(\\ell'\\); in particular \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are covered. No other point loses a covering because \\(\\ell\\) contributed only points with second coordinate \\(b_{1}\\).  \n\n2. **Both points have the same first coordinate** (\\(a_{1}=a_{2}\\)).  \n   Replace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell' :\\; x=a_{1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny. The same argument as in case\u202f1 shows that the covering property is preserved.\n\n3. **The two points differ in both coordinates** (\\(a_{1}\\neq a_{2}\\) and \\(b_{1}\\neq b_{2}\\)).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) passes through two distinct points, its equation can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   y-b_{1}=m\\,(x-a_{1}),\\qquad m\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The equality of slopes for the two points gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging yields  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence both points have the same sum \\(c:=a_{1}+b_{1}\\).  \n   Replace \\(\\ell\\) by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell' :\\; x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   This line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), thus non\u2011sunny. It contains **all** points of (0.1) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\); in particular it contains \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). No other point loses a covering because any point previously covered only by \\(\\ell\\) must have sum \\(c\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nIn each case \\(\\ell'\\) is non\u2011sunny, distinct from all lines already present (the three families of parallel lines are mutually disjoint because the original configuration contains at most one line of each forbidden direction), and the covering condition (0.1) remains true. \u220e\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4.\u202fInduction on the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) there exists an admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case.*  \nThe construction of Section\u202f2 provides a family with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{r}\\) with \\(k=n-3-r\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le r<n-3\\)) has been built. If \\(r=n-3\\) we are done (the family has no sunny lines). Otherwise \\(\\mathcal L_{r}\\) contains at least one sunny line. Apply Lemma\u202f2 to replace one sunny line by a non\u2011sunny line, obtaining a new family \\(\\mathcal L_{r+1}\\) with exactly one fewer sunny line, i.e. with \\(k=n-3-(r+1)\\) sunny lines. Distinctness of the lines is preserved, and condition (0.1) continues to hold.\n\nBy iterating the replacement \\(r\\) times we obtain families with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n-3\\).\n\n### Small\u2011\\(n\\) verification  \n\n*For \\(n=3\\):*  \nThe set (0.1) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\). The three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\; x=1,\\; x+y=3\n\\]\n\ncover them, giving \\(k=0\\). Lemma\u202f1 yields \\(k\\le0\\); thus the only admissible value is \\(0\\).\n\n*For \\(n=4\\):*  \nThe set (0.1) has six points. The construction of Section\u202f2 gives three non\u2011sunny lines as above and one sunny line  \n\n\\[\nU_{2}: y=-2x+6,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\). Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable. Applying Lemma\u202f2 to the sunny line replaces it by, say, the horizontal line \\(y=2\\), producing a configuration with \\(k=0\\). Lemma\u202f1 gives the upper bound \\(k\\le1\\). Thus the admissible values are \\(0,1\\).\n\nThus the induction works for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5.\u202fConclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nSection\u202f2 constructs a configuration attaining the maximal value \\(k=n-3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides