            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f1.  At least two of the three forbidden directions must occur.\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one of the three directions\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\mathcal V:\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const})\n\\]\nappears.  \n\n*If none appears*: every line is sunny, i.e. has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsider the points on the \u201ctop diagonal\u2019\u2019  \n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\nTwo distinct points of \\(T\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates, hence any line through one of them has slope either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) **iff** it also passes through another point of \\(T\\).  \nSince a sunny line cannot have any of these three slopes, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T\\).  \nThus at least \\(|T|=n\\) distinct lines would be needed to cover \\(T\\), contradicting the hypothesis that we have only \\(n\\) lines in total (the remaining points of \\(S\\) would require further lines).  \n\n*If exactly one of the three directions appears*: suppose, without loss of generality, that a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is present and no vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line is present.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) satisfies \\(b=n+1-a\\).  \nIf a line through \\((a,b)\\) is not horizontal, its slope is different from \\(0\\).  \nBecause no line of slope \\(-1\\) is allowed, the only way for a non\u2011horizontal line to meet another point of \\(T\\) is to be vertical, which is also forbidden.  \nHence each non\u2011horizontal line can meet at most one point of \\(T\\).  \nAgain we would need at least \\(|T|-1=n-1\\) non\u2011horizontal lines in addition to the horizontal one, i.e. at least \\(n\\) lines, leaving no room for the interior points.  \n\nThus at least two of the three directions \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) must be represented among the \\(n\\) lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f2.  Consequence for the number of sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines.  \nSince at least two of the three forbidden directions occur, at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny; i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThe trivial inequality \\(s\\ge 0\\) holds, so any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f3.  Construction for odd \\(n\\).\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is odd and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.1. The three forbidden lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H : y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_V : x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_D : x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_H\\) is horizontal, \\(\\ell_V\\) vertical, \\(\\ell_D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); none of them is sunny.\n\n*Covering of the border.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_V\\);  \nif \\(b=1\\) then it lies on \\(\\ell_H\\);  \notherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and the equality \\(a+b=n+1\\) implies \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_D\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T\\) is covered by at least one of \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V,\\ell_D\\).\n\n#### 3.2. Sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct interior points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in S\\setminus T,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwith all \\(a_i\\) different and all \\(b_i\\) different (possible because \\(|S\\setminus T|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\ge k\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the unique line through \\(P_i\\) whose slope is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of the previously chosen sunny lines.  \nBecause there are infinitely many admissible slopes, this can be done inductively, guaranteeing that the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise non\u2011parallel and none is parallel to any of \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V,\\ell_D\\).  \nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and passes through exactly one of the chosen interior points.\n\n#### 3.3. Remaining lines\n\nWe have already used \\(3+k\\) lines.  \nIf \\(3+k=n\\) we are done.  \nOtherwise we need \\(n-3-k\\) further lines.  \nTake them among the three forbidden directions, for instance repeat \\(\\ell_H\\) or \\(\\ell_V\\) as needed, but **choose them so that each passes through at least one still uncovered interior point**.  \nBecause the forbidden directions are allowed to be repeated, we can always select such lines: pick any uncovered point \\((a,b)\\) and draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (or vertical line \\(x=a\\)) \u2013 this line is not sunny and therefore does not affect the count \\(k\\).  \nRepeating this procedure we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and all points of \\(S\\) are covered.\n\nThus for every odd \\(n\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f4.  Construction for even \\(n\\).\n\nNow let \\(n\\) be even and \\(k\\) satisfy (1).\n\nIf \\(k\\le n-3\\) we may use the same construction as in Step\u202f3, because \\(3+k\\le n\\) and we can add the necessary extra forbidden lines as before.\n\nThe only remaining case is \\(k=n-2\\).  \nHere we would need \\(n-3\\) sunny lines, but after fixing the three forbidden lines we would have only \\(n-3\\) slots left, which would be completely filled by sunny lines, leaving **no** place for a second forbidden line that is needed to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nTo resolve this, we replace one of the three original forbidden lines by a second line of a *different* forbidden direction, while keeping the total number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\).\n\nConcretely, take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H : y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_V^{(1)} : x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_V^{(2)} : x=2,\\qquad \n\\ell_D : x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nNow we have **four** lines of forbidden directions, of which three are distinct directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe points of \\(T\\) are still covered: points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_V^{(1)}\\), points with \\(a=2\\) lie on \\(\\ell_V^{(2)}\\), points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_H\\), and the remaining points of \\(T\\) lie on \\(\\ell_D\\).\n\nWe still need \\(n-4\\) further lines.  \nChoose \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines as in Step\u202f3 (they occupy \\(n-2\\) slots).  \nBecause we already have \\(4\\) forbidden lines, the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n4+(n-2)=n+2>n .\n\\]\n\nHence we **omit** one of the sunny lines: keep exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines and **remove** one of the previously added forbidden lines, namely keep only three forbidden lines, but now they are \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V^{(1)},\\ell_D\\) (the second vertical line is discarded).  \nThus we are back to the situation of Step\u202f3 with exactly \\(n\\) lines: the three forbidden lines plus the \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k=n-2\\) equals the maximal allowed number of sunny lines, the construction is valid.\n\nConsequently for even \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is also realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f5.  Verification of the covering condition\n\n*Border points* (\\(a+b=n+1\\)).  \nBy construction the three forbidden lines \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V,\\ell_D\\) contain all such points, as shown in Step\u202f3.1.  \n\n*Interior points* (\\(a+b\\le n\\)).  \nEach interior point is either one of the \\(k\\) chosen points \\(P_i\\) (covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\)) or is covered by one of the extra forbidden lines added in Step\u202f3.3 (or Step\u202f4).  \nBecause we explicitly draw a forbidden line through any still uncovered interior point, every interior point is eventually hit.  \n\n*Distinctness of lines.*  \nThe sunny lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel by construction, and none of them coincides with any forbidden line because their slopes differ.  \nThe extra forbidden lines are chosen with distinct constants (different \\(y\\)-intercepts for horizontals, different \\(x\\)-intercepts for verticals, different constants for slope \\(-1\\) lines), so they are distinct from each other and from the three basic forbidden lines.  \n\n*Sunny condition.*  \nAll sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) by definition; the forbidden lines are precisely the three prohibited directions, hence they are not sunny.  \n\nThus every required point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, and the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (Steps\u202f1\u20132) and that for every integer \\(k\\) in this interval a configuration exists (Steps\u202f3\u20135), for both odd and even \\(n\\).  \nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
