            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Three forced non\u2011sunny directions\n\nThe three sides of the triangle are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{h}&:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nS_{v}&:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\nS_{d}&:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}\\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) contains two distinct points of a side, then \\(L\\) is parallel to that side, because a side is itself a line segment.  \nEach side contains at least two points of \\(T_{n}\\) (e.g. \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) on \\(S_{h}\\)), therefore any covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines must contain a line parallel to each side.  \n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) necessarily contains\n\n* a horizontal line,\n* a vertical line,\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThese three lines are **not** sunny by definition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Counting sunny lines after the forced ones\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nChoose one horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\in\\mathcal L\\), one vertical line \\(L_{v}\\in\\mathcal L\\) and one line of slope \\(-1\\), say \\(L_{d}\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe remaining set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\prime}:=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_{h},L_{v},L_{d}\\}\n\\]\n\nhas exactly \\(n-3\\) elements.  \n\nA line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\prime}\\) is sunny **iff** its direction is different from the three forbidden directions.  \nHence\n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L^{\\prime}\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}\n\\]\n\nand therefore  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Necessary range for \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (1) we already have the upper bound \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nBecause the three forced lines are always present, at least three lines are non\u2011sunny; thus the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at least \\(3\\).  \nIf \\(k=n-2\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines would be \\(2\\) or \\(1\\), contradicting the forced three.  \nHence the values \\(k=n-2\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the two observations we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy (2).  \nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **The three forced lines** (non\u2011sunny):\n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}: y=1,\\qquad L_{v}: x=1,\\qquad L_{d}: x+y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(L_{h}\\)* contains all points of \\(S_{h}\\); *\\(L_{v}\\)* contains all points of \\(S_{v}\\); *\\(L_{d}\\)* contains all points of \\(S_{d}\\).\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{1}{i+1}\\,(x- (i+1)) + 2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the interior lattice point \\((i+2,2)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{1}{i+1}\\), which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \n   Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct slopes, hence the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are pairwise non\u2011parallel and none of them is parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; consequently they are sunny.\n\n3. **Remaining \\(n-3-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   If \\(n-3-k>0\\) we add extra lines parallel to the three forbidden directions, choosing intercepts that have not been used before, for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &y=2,\\; y=3,\\dots ,y=2+(n-3-k)_{h},\\\\\n   &x=2,\\; x=3,\\dots ,x=2+(n-3-k)_{v},\\\\\n   &x+y=n+1,\\; x+y=n+2,\\dots ,x+y=n+(n-3-k)_{d},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   where the non\u2011negative integers \\((n-3-k)_{h},(n-3-k)_{v},(n-3-k)_{d}\\) are chosen so that their sum equals \\(n-3-k\\).  \n   All these lines are clearly non\u2011sunny and distinct from each other and from the three forced lines.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n3\\;(\\text{forced})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-3-k)\\;(\\text{extra non\u2011sunny})=n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  Verification of the covering property\n\nWe must show that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,1)\\in S_{h}\\) and is on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\in S_{v}\\) and is on \\(L_{v}\\).  \n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in S_{d}\\) and is on \\(L_{d}\\).\n\nThus all points on the three sides are covered.\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\) be an interior point, i.e. \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b\\le n-a\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose \\(i=b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) whenever \\(b\\le k+1\\)).  \n\nFrom the definition (3) we compute the value of \\(y\\) on \\(L_{i}\\) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{L_{i}}(a)=\\frac{1}{i+1}\\,(a-(i+1))+2\n            =\\frac{a-i-1}{i+1}+2 .\n\\]\n\nIf we set \\(i=b-1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{L_{b-1}}(a)=\\frac{a-b}{b}+2\n             =\\frac{a-b+2b}{b}\n             =\\frac{a+b}{b}= \\frac{a+b}{b}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\), the right\u2011hand side equals exactly \\(b\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b-1}\\).  \n\nIf \\(b-1>k\\) (i.e. \\(b\\ge k+2\\)), then \\(b\\) is at most \\(n\\) and we can use one of the extra non\u2011sunny lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with suitable constant \\(c\\) (chosen among the extra lines in Step\u202f4) to cover the point; indeed the set of extra lines \\(x+y=n+1,\\dots ,x+y=n+(n-3-k)_{d}\\) contains a line with constant \\(c=a+b\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\le n+(n-3-k)_{d}\\).  \n\nThus every interior lattice point is covered as well.\n\nAll lines are distinct by construction: the three forced lines have distinct directions; the sunny lines have distinct slopes; the extra non\u2011sunny lines have either a different direction or a different intercept from any previously used line.\n\nConsequently the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies both required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Edge Cases\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  The triangle \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\).  \n  The forced lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=3\\) already cover all three points, and there are no remaining lines (\\(n-3=0\\)). Hence the only possible value is \\(k=0\\), which agrees with the formula \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n-3\\).**  \n  In this case all free lines are sunny. The construction uses the \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes (3) and no extra non\u2011sunny lines; the covering argument above still works because every interior point has \\(b\\le k+1=n-2\\) and is therefore hit by a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any covering family must contain at least one horizontal, one vertical and one slope \\(-1\\) line, so at least three lines are non\u2011sunny.\n* Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) in this interval we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
